<div class="learn-box">
**What you will learn**

Build education and cause-of-death columns, check language 2, and request category counts.
</div>

This chapter uses the 24-person synthetic questionnaire test fixture described in [Getting started](book-00-start.html).
**You need:** the packages and the matching practice connection described in
[Before you start](book-00-start.html#before-you-start).
Run the sections in order in one R session. All printed results below were computed
with dsOMOP and dsOMOPClient 2.10.5. They are teaching data, not clinical findings.

Concept selections refer to the searches and saved definitions in
[chapter 4](book-04-vocabulary.html), including the separate questionnaire
catalogue. Additional body-measurement concepts are found on screen in the
[formula walkthrough](book-15-formulas.html#find-the-inputs-before-calculating).

## Start this chapter

Start a fresh R session in the `book-work` directory created in
[Getting started](book-00-start.html#get-the-book-helpers). Run this setup first:

```{r chapter-setup}
library(dsOMOPClient)
source("tools/book/display.R")
source("tools/book/fixture.R")
practice <- book_fixture()
conns <- practice$conns
names(conns)
```

The `site` connection is ready. This chapter creates its temporary questionnaire fixture locally.

## Select the education question

An *event* describes records of interest. It is a definition on your computer,
not a downloaded table. We have switched from chapter 4's GiBleed asthma search
to the questionnaire fixture. First look up its education question by concept:

```{r education-question}
question <- ds.omop.concept.lookup(900001L, conns = conns)
knitr::kable(question$pooled["concept_name"])
```

Now use that question to select observations:

```{r education-event}
education_event <- omop_event("observation", concepts = 900001L)
class(education_event)
```

The server will select observations whose question is Education question.

## Choose one answer per person

A *reducer* turns possibly many records into one value. Use the latest nonmissing
coded answer, ordered by the observation date:

```{r education-variable}
education <- omop_variable(
  event = education_event,
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"))
class(education)
```

This makes one categorical column. It does not create a separate binary column
for every education level. A same-date tie uses the documented deterministic
record ordering; it does not mean “largest answer ID”.

## Add cause of death

```{r cause-variable}
cause <- omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("death"),
  reducer = omop_reduce("first_value", value = "cause_concept_id"))
class(cause)
```

Here the value comes from `cause_concept_id`. Do not substitute `cause_source_value`:
that is free source text, not the curated coded cause used by this route.

## Describe a wide table

*Wide* means one column per variable. *Person grain* means one row per person.
The native all-person population retains people who have neither answer nor death.

```{r people-recipe}
people <- omop_recipe(
  variables = list(education = education, cause_of_death = cause),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
class(people)
```

`person_id` is included automatically as a pseudonym. Missing records produce
missing values. We did not add a legacy raw-ID variable to keep the roster.

## Predict: read the recipe explanation

```{r explain}
explained <- recipe_explain(people, conns = conns)
stopifnot(explained$selected == "v2")
explained$selected
```

The selected plan is language 2. Before running, predict one private row per
person, two named categorical variables, and a pseudonymous key. With no time restriction, each variable reads all available history
independently. That is a teaching definition to choose explicitly in a real study.

<details><summary>Full plan explanation (optional)</summary>

The semantic version identifies the rules used to interpret the recipe. A label
contract records the category names and their ordering. Federation reconciliation
makes those categories agree across sites. A bytewise tie compares the bytes of
text to make a repeated choice deterministic. You do not need to configure these
for this first recipe.

Read the lines in order: the selected language is the execution route; the
population defines the row; each variable identifies its source and reducer;
output defaults determine the shape; category reconciliation determines names.
The explanation contains declarations, not observed patient frequencies.

Print all the chosen defaults:

```{r explain-full}
recipe_explain(people)
```

</details>

## Run: assign the result

```{r execute}
print(recipe_execute(people, out = c(people = "D_people"), conns = conns))
```

The reply is a mapping of output names to server symbols. `D_people` is still on
the server. Concept-valued columns are labelled factors because of their declared
type. In 2.10.5 the names `education` and `cause_of_death` need no `_concept_id` suffix.

## Verify: check what you got

Do this complete check once while learning a new extraction. `ds.ls` lists
server symbols; `ds.dim` describes the result dimensions; `ds.colnames` names
its columns; `ds.class` checks types. Then inspect one summary or table and
missingness with `ds.numNA`. Every method applies its own release rules. None
of these calls retrieves patient rows.

```{r full-output-check}
dsBaseClient::ds.ls(datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.dim("D_people", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_people", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.class("D_people$education", datasources = conns)
dsBaseClient::ds.numNA("D_people$education", datasources = conns)
```

The category table in the next step completes the check. Compare it with your
prediction. Later lessons use at most one helpful schema or variable check per
extraction; type checks recur only when the lesson is about types. Explain a
recipe again when a new default, censoring, matching or presentation rule matters.
This is the **predict, run, verify** habit of a careful federated analyst.

## Count the education categories

```{r education-table}
education_table_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_people$education", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(education_table_result))
```

<details><summary>Full aggregate response</summary>

```{r education-table-full}
education_table_result
```

</details>

The Count column answers how many people have each answer. The full response
also contains proportions (fractions of a row or column total); we select its
`TABLES.COMBINED_all.sources_counts` component after checking that every site
released a valid result. The helper stops on a withheld or incomplete result.
The labels are names, not numeric codes. `useNA = "no"` excludes missing answers;
the counts therefore need not add up to the whole population. DataSHIELD checks
whether cells may be released before returning the table.

## Count causes

```{r cause-table}
ds.omop.table("D_people", "cause_of_death", conns = conns)
```

`ds.omop.table()` calls the real dsBase `ds.table()` and preserves the complete
published category contract, including declared zero categories. It also protects
labels containing punctuation. Prefer it when you need that complete contract.

## Give a question your own yes/no labels

```{r fever}
fever <- omop_variable(
  event = omop_event("observation", concepts = 900002L),
  reducer = omop_reduce("last_value", value = "value_as_concept_id"),
  labels = c("900301" = "yes", "900302" = "no"))
class(fever)
```

The map is keyed by answer concepts. It changes presentation, not which question
is selected. Missing fever answers remain missing; they do not become “no”.

```{r answers}
answers <- omop_recipe(
  variables = list(education = education, fever = fever, cause_of_death = cause),
  outputs = omop_output("people", format = "wide", grain = "person"))
print(recipe_execute(answers, out = c(people = "D_answers"), conns = conns))
```

```{r yes-no}
yes_no_result <- dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_answers$fever", useNA = "no", datasources = conns)
knitr::kable(book_counts(yes_no_result))
```

<details><summary>Full aggregate response</summary>

```{r yes-no-full}
yes_no_result
```

</details>

The table contains your lower-case labels. See [chapter 10](book-10-harmonise.html)
for the same operation across two different vocabularies.

## Try it

Count education using the complete-contract helper instead of calling ds.table directly.

<details><summary>Solution</summary>


```{r solution}
ds.omop.table("D_people", "education", conns = conns)
```

</details>

## Exercise 2: a common variant

Count causes with dsBase directly, preserving missing causes.

<details><summary>Solution 2</summary>

```{r book4-exercise-2}
knitr::kable(book_counts(dsBaseClient::ds.table("D_people$cause_of_death", useNA = "always", datasources = conns)))
```

Missing cause is not another coded cause and not proof that a person survived.

</details>

## Exercise 3: a pitfall to avoid

Verify the combined answer table has the three intended variables.

<details><summary>Solution 3</summary>

```{r book4-exercise-3}
dsBaseClient::ds.colnames("D_answers", datasources = conns)
```

The pseudonymous person key is automatic. The education, fever and cause columns come from separate declarations.

</details>
## Recap

An event selects records, a reducer selects a value, and a wide output arranges private rows. Only the aggregate returns to you.

**Next:** [6. Events, windows and reducers](book-06-events.html).
